content.text:
Zed is the likely cause, then. Zed runs rust-analyzer, which runs `cargo check` on save in the same `target/` directory, without our variables. Each time it does, our next build recompiles the crate.

The fix is to give rust-analyzer its own target directory. Add this to your Zed settings, merging the `lsp` key into any existing one:

```json
"lsp": {
  "rust-analyzer": {
    "initialization_options": {
      "cargo": {
        "targetDir": true
      }
    }
  }
}
```

With `true`, rust-analyzer builds into `target/rust-analyzer` instead of sharing `target/debug`. The two stop invalidating each other. Zed should restart rust-analyzer when the settings change. If it doesn't, run "editor: restart language server". The first check afterward is a cold build of that separate directory, so expect one slow pass.

I'd put it in your user settings, `~/.config/zed/settings.json`, rather than the repo. It's an editor preference on one machine, and the sandbox doesn't let me write there anyway. If you'd rather every clone get it, the same block in a project `.zed/settings.json` would work. That would be a repo change, though, so tell me and I'll add it to this rung.

The cost of doing nothing is small: about 1.8 seconds on the next build after each save, with correctness unaffected.

